MAYOR OF BUKITTINGGI

PROVINCE OF WEST SUMATRA

REGIONAL REGULATION OF BUKITTINGGI CITY

NUMBER 4 OF 2015

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF BUKITTINGGI,

Considering:
a. that in order to guarantee and protect the rights of women and children so that they can participate optimally in accordance with human dignity and honor, and receive protection from violence, discrimination and violations, protection efforts need to be carried out;
b. that in order for the protection efforts for women and children to obtain optimal results, there needs to be concrete action from the regional government and community participation;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Protection of Women and Children;

In View of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 9 of 1956 concerning the Establishment of Autonomous Regions of Large Cities Within the Provincial Region of Central Sumatra (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1956 Number 20);
3. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019);
4. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3243);
5. Law Number 8 of 1981 concerning Criminal Procedure Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1981 Number 76, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3209);
6. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (Convention On The Elimination Of All Forms Of Discrimination Against Women) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29);
7. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
8. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
9. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);
10. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
11. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
12. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 11, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
13. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formation of Laws and Regulations (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
14. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
15. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning the Division of Government Affairs Between the Government, Provincial Regional Government and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Improving the Quality of Life of Women;
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;
18. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
19. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 15 of 2010 concerning General Guidelines for Handling Children in Conflict with the Law;
20. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Handling of Child Victims of Violence;
21. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 1 of 2014 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 32;
22. Regional Regulation of Bukittinggi City Number 6 of 2000 concerning Civil Servant Investigators (Regional Gazette of Bukittinggi City of 2000 Number 7);
23. Regional Regulation of Bukittinggi City Number 11 of 2008 concerning the Establishment of Organization and Work Procedures of Regional Technical Institutions of Bukittinggi City (Regional Gazette of Bukittinggi City of 2008 Number 11), as amended by Regional Regulation of Bukittinggi City Number 9 of 2013 (Regional Gazette of Bukittinggi City of 2013 Number 9).

With Mutual Approval REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF BUKITTINGGI CITY And

Article 4

Every woman has the right to:
a. Obtain protection from discrimination, exploitation (economic and social), neglect, cruelty, violence, abuse (physical, psychological, and sexual) injustice and other wrongful treatment.  b. Obtain equal opportunities and rights with men as human beings, in order to be able to play a role and participate in political, economic, social and cultural activities, defense and security and equality in enjoying the results of development.  c. For working women:
1. obtain leave/rest for 1.5 (one and a half) months before the time of childbirth and 1.5 (one and a half) months after childbirth according to the calculation of an obstetrician or midwife, while still receiving wages or salary in accordance with applicable laws and regulations;
2. obtain leave/rest for 1.5 (one and a half) months in the event of a miscarriage while still receiving wages or salary in accordance with applicable laws and regulations;
3. obtain appropriate opportunities to provide breast milk to her child if this must be done during working hours, while still receiving wages or salary in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.  d. Every woman who is married has the right to receive material and spiritual support from her husband.  e. Every woman who is left by her husband due to a divorce process must be provided with material support by her husband until there is a court decision in accordance with statutory regulations.  f. Every woman has the right to obtain decent work in accordance with her dignity and nature.  g. Obtain knowledge and skills that are useful for improving self-quality and family welfare.

Article 10

1. Protection of children starts from the womb until the age of 18 (eighteen) years.
2. Protection of children in the womb, infants and toddlers includes:
a. protection of children in the womb, including ensuring children are born in a healthy condition, to reduce maternal mortality rates and reduce child mortality rates;
b. ensuring every child is born through a medical process in accordance with SOPs from the Ministry of Health;
c. ensuring every child after birth is conducted with religious guidance professed by their parents;
d. provision of complete immunization facilities at posyandu, puskesmas or government hospitals;
e. improvement of quality and competence of cadres at Posyandu, to ensure correct procedures in order to guarantee the health of mothers and children including the immunization process;
f. provision of proper facilities for every posyandu and Toddler Family Development (BKB) in every kelurahan;
g. ensuring the quality of knowledge of posyandu cadres and toddler family development cadres in accordance with government standards;
h. providing education to the community about becoming great parents, to ensure child health, both at posyandu, toddler family development together with government and community work partners;
i. provision of playgrounds and childcare facilities managed by the regional government or organizations under the auspices of the Regional Government;
j. provision of Breastfeeding Corners in offices that have female workers; and
k. provision of attention and affection from the family.
(3) Protection for school-age children:
a. prohibiting acts of bullying against children at school;
b. ensuring there are no acts of bullying between children, teachers to children, parents to children and the community to children whether at school, at home or in the midst of the community;
c. providing education to schools, families and the community about child-friendly schools;
d. requiring every school at all levels to meet child-friendly school criteria;
e. receiving full attention and affection from family and community;
f. receiving religious guidance and local customs/culture including sumbang 12 (dua baleh);
g. receiving twelve years of compulsory education;
h. providing scholarship programs for underprivileged children and children who excel;
i. providing supporting facilities for extracurricular activities such as for sports subjects, arts and other local content as well as activity supervisory teachers; and
j. prohibiting school children from being in internet cafes during school hours and after 18.00 WIB.
4. Every person and/or any party is obliged to protect school-age children from acts of violence in the family, school and community environment.
5. Protection of neglected children:
a. regional government, NGOs/social organizations and the community are obliged to provide protection to neglected children domiciled in the Region in accordance with laws and regulations;
b. the regional government ensures children do not live on the streets;
c. protection for neglected children whose parents do not have the ability and/or commit neglect as well as children who do not have parents, which is implemented through institutional and non-institutional services by the regional government and the community;
d. the form of institutional services as referred to in letter (c), is implemented in a family atmosphere and in the child's nearest environment managed by the community and in the form of an institution;
e. the form of non-institutional services as referred to in letter (c) is implemented in a family atmosphere managed by the community and not in the form of an institution; and
f. community-owned orphanages/shelters as referred to in letter (d) must meet the requirements in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

Article 30

1. KLA development policy is directed at the fulfillment of children's rights which are divided into 5 (five) groups.
2. The fulfillment of children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1), consists of:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;
d. education and child-friendly schools, use of leisure time, and cultural activities; and
e. special protection.
3. The fulfillment of children's rights as referred to in paragraph (2), its mechanism and implementation are further regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 31

1. In order to ensure the effectiveness of the implementation of KLA policy in the Region, a KLA Task Force is established.
2. The implementation of KLA policy as referred to in paragraph (1), is stipulated by Mayor's Decree.
3. The KLA Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1), has the main duties of:
a. coordinating the implementation of KLA policy and development;
b. determining the tasks of Task Force members;
c. conducting socialization, advocacy and communication, information and education of KLA policy;
d. collecting baseline data;
e. conducting needs analysis sourced from baseline data;
f. disseminating baseline data;
g. determining the focus and priority of programs in realizing KLA, which are adjusted to regional potential;
h. preparing a 5 (five) year regional action plan for KLA and work mechanisms; and
i. conducting monitoring, evaluation and reporting at least once (1) a year.
4. Membership of the KLA Task Force is stipulated by Mayor's Decree.
